//! Go-pinned Unicode character classes.
//!
//! GENERATED FILE — DO NOT EDIT BY HAND. Regenerate with the throwaway Go
//! program documented in `tests/fixtures/README.md`.
//!
//! 🚨 Why this exists. Rust's Unicode predicates are STRICTLY WIDER than Go's
//! equivalents, so every naive port silently classifies more characters as
//! word/letter than production does. Measured over all 1,112,064 Unicode
//! scalar values (rustc 1.97.1 / regex 1.13 vs Go go1.23.6):
//!
//! | Rust                    | Go                        | differing code points |
//! |-------------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------|
//! | `char::is_alphanumeric` | `IsLetter \|\| IsDigit`     | 12,322 (`² ³ ¹ ¼ ½ ¾ ①②③`) |
//! | `char::is_alphabetic`   | `unicode.IsLetter`          | 11,317 |
//! | `char::is_numeric`      | `unicode.IsDigit`           | 1,244 |
//! | literal `\p{L}`         | RE2 `\p{L}`                 | 4,924 (newer tables) |
//!
//! Rust is wider in every case and Go is never wider, so a naive port accepts
//! characters production rejects. Pinning to the tables below makes the port
//! version-proof: it keeps matching production as `regex` and rustc advance.
//! `tests/goclass_all_scalars.rs` re-proves the pinning over every scalar.
//!
//! Provenance: Go unicode.Version 15.0.0, 705 word-char ranges, 659 letter ranges.
//! [`LETTER_RANGES`] is read off the COMPILED
//! `regexp.MustCompile("^\\p{L}$")` rather than the `unicode` tables, so it is
//! exactly the class RE2 applies.

